# Vulnerability details

## The function `StableVault.withdraw` has the risk of of stealing `_token` tokens
The function `StableVault.withdraw` does a swap from `tigAsset` to `_token`. Then it is expected to burn `tigAsset` in exchange for token `_token`. However the function itself doesn't check if the `_msgSender()`  has enough balance of `stable` tokens to burn. And the ERC20 Mintable implementation doesn't have the implementation for `burnFrom` as we can see, for example, OpenZeppelin's (OZ) implementation [`Here`](`https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts/blob/9b3710465583284b8c4c5d2245749246bb2e0094/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20Mintable.sol`). The OpenZeppelin's (OZ) ERC20Burnable implementation, however, has the `burnFrom` which checks the balance of the account before burning the tokens and it reverse in case the balance is not enough. But since there is no way to garantee that calling `burnFrom` in the function `StableVault.withdraw` will revert in case there is not enough balance, any external user could keep withrawing tokens without burning the counterpart.

## File: StableVault.sol#L65
[`StableVault.sol#L65`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-tigris/blob/588c84b7bb354d20cbca6034544c4faa46e6a80e/contracts/StableVault.sol#L65)

```solidity
 function withdraw(address _token, uint256 _amount) external returns (uint256 _output) {
        IERC20Mintable(stable).burnFrom(_msgSender(), _amount);
        _output = _amount/10**(18-IERC20Mintable(_token).decimals());
        IERC20(_token).transfer(
            _msgSender(),
            _output
        );
    }
```

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Ensure the `burnFrom` reverts in case of not enough balance of `stable` tokens to burn. The implementation of `burnFrom` would be the one of OZ `ERC20Burnable` instead of `ERC20Mintable` or any other implementation th

