# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In function `addToPosition()`, new margin amount is added to existing position. This margin asset is added to position immediately to increase position size. Since they are added at different times so they have different prices, and there is only one slot for price in Position metadata. So we have to take weighed average of previous price and current price as open price for the whole position.

In function `addToPosition()`, it did exactly what I've just described
```solidity
uint _newMargin = _trade.margin + _addMargin;
uint _newPrice = _trade.price*_trade.margin/_newMargin + _price*_addMargin/_newMargin;
```

However, it used margin amount as the weights in its formula, which is wrong because `margin` of position can be changed without changing the price when using functions `addMargin()` or `removeMargin()`. As you can see below, it only modify margin and leverage, but not the price.

```solidity
function addMargin(
    uint256 _id,
    address _marginAsset,
    address _stableVault,
    uint256 _addMargin,
    ERC20PermitData calldata _permitData,
    address _trader
)
    external
{
    ...
    unchecked {
        uint256 _newMargin = _trade.margin + _addMargin;
        uint256 _newLeverage = _trade.margin * _trade.leverage / _newMargin;
        if (_newLeverage < asset.minLeverage) revert("!lev");
        position.modifyMargin(_id, _newMargin, _newLeverage);
        emit MarginModified(_id, _newMargin, _newLeverage, true, _trader);
    }
}
```

The result is users can abused this wrong formula, calling `addMargin()` to increase margin value and call `addToPosition()` to manipulate the openning price of position.

