# Vulnerability details

### Impact

The inconsistency in different ERC20 having different decimal places can lead to complete drainage of the StableVault contract.

### POC

The `StableVault` has a functionality to list new tokens. Let’s say in future there are two allowed tokens in StableVault:

- USDC - ( 6 decimal places )
- DAI - ( 18 decimal places )

Now depositing 1 USDC token would mint `1e12` stable coints.

```solidity
function deposit(address _token, uint256 _amount) public {
        require(allowed[_token], "Token not listed");
        IERC20(_token).transferFrom(_msgSender(), address(this), _amount);
        IERC20Mintable(stable).mintFor(
            _msgSender(),
            _amount*(10**(18-IERC20Mintable(_token).decimals())) // [Note] as usdc decimals would return 6, value would be 1*(10**(18-6))
        );
    }
```



Withdrawing those stable coins using `withdraw` function and using DAI as the _token parameter would now send `1e12` DAI tokens

```solidity
function withdraw(address _token, uint256 _amount) external returns (uint256 _output) {
        IERC20Mintable(stable).burnFrom(_msgSender(), _amount);
        _output = _amount/10**(18-IERC20Mintable(_token).decimals()); // [Note] DAI decimals would return 18. Value would be 1e12/10**(18-18) = 1e12
        IERC20(_token).transfer(
            _msgSender(),
            _output
        );
    }
```

So the attacker got `1e12` DAI tokens just by depositing `1` USDC token. 

### Recommendations:

While listing a token, make sure that the token uses correct decimal points.

