# Vulnerability details

## Impact
tigUSD will continue to be minted, and all the funds in the vaults will be taken away in exchange for the stable coin in the vaults.

## Proof of Concept
```
    function cancelLimitOrder(
        uint256 _id,
        address _trader
    )
        external
    {
        _validateProxy(_trader);
        _checkOwner(_id, _trader);
        IPosition.Trade memory _trade = position.trades(_id);
        if (_trade.orderType == 0) revert();
        IStable(_trade.tigAsset).mintFor(_trader, _trade.margin);
        position.burn(_id);
        emit LimitCancelled(_id, _trader);
    }
```
The `cancelLimitOrder` function is a function that cancels a specified order. But the problem is that the tigAsset is minted first and the position is burned later. An attacker can open a position using a malicious contract and then continue minting tigUSD through a reentrancy attack when calling `cancelLimitOrder`.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
```
    function cancelLimitOrder(
        uint256 _id,
        address _trader
    )
        external
    {
        _validateProxy(_trader);
        _checkOwner(_id, _trader);
        IPosition.Trade memory _trade = position.trades(_id);
        if (_trade.orderType == 0) revert();
        position.burn(_id);
        IStable(_trade.tigAsset).mintFor(_trader, _trade.margin);
        emit LimitCancelled(_id, _trader);
    }
```
The position should be burned before the tigAsset is minted. It is recommended to use the `nonReentrant` modifier in the function through ReentrancyGuard.sol to ensure that the function is not reentered.

